{\plain {\fs24 \ul \b Phase Shift Keying/Phase
 Reversal Keying}}
}
.TXT 3 0 120 0
Cg a71.300000,73.000000,361
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {Digital information can be transmitted
 using }{a phase modulation method called
 }{phase shift keying,  This invloves mapping
 digital symbols into phase angles.  We 
are specifically considering phase reversal
 keying in which a binary sequence is evaluated
 one bit at a time and each bit is mapped
 onto phase angles that switch between 0
 and 180 degrees.   }}
}
.TXT 10 0 82 0
Cg a38.700000,73.000000,56
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {\fs24 \ul \b Define Signal Transmission
 / Modulation }}
}
.TXT 3 0 121 0
Cg a72.300000,72.000000,424
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {The input signal }{\b Seq}{ represents
 our binary sequence, a pattern of alternating
 logic 1's and 0's.  This sequence is obtained
 by randomly picking numbers in the interval
 (0..X), where X is any number, and testing
 whether the choosen number is above  X/2
 or below X/2.  If the choosen }{number}{
 is below X/2 then we assign this value 
to be a logic 1 otherwise, it is assigned
 a logic 0.  Here we choose X to be 5.}}
}
.TXT 9 1 193 0
Cg a36.800000,72.000000,59
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
}
{\plain {\ul Digital Sequence and its time
 domain representation.}}
}
